  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDry weather today will come to an end early tomorrow morning as\na broad upper level trough and accompanying surface low \npressure track east into the Lower Great Lakes and Ohio Valley \nregion. Moisture transport from the Gulf will increase area \nPWATs on Wednesday to around 1.50\" which is above the 90th \npercentile for early November at regional sites. Widespread \nshowers are expected to enter from the west Wednesday morning \nand push eastward through the day before tapering off from west \nto east in the evening. Can't rule out a few isolated embedded \nthunderstorms tomorrow as SBCAPE values may briefly increase to \na few hundred J/kg in the morning and early afternoon. Severe \nthreat remains limited given the lower forecasted instability \namount. Overall rainfall amounts will range between 0.25-0.50 \ninches for much of the forecast area with locally higher amounts\nof 0.50-0.75 inches most likely along and south of US-30. \nAdditionally, a 40-50 LLJ will move overhead tomorrow and allow \nfor southerly wind gusts to increase to 25-30 MPH. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThere will likely be a window of dry weather on Thursday as the\ntrough lifts north before another trough sinks south Thursday \nnight into Friday. This secondary trough will lead to occasional\nchances for showers downwind of Lake Erie through the weekend \nand into early next week. Cooler temperatures expected this \nweekend with highs in the 50s and overnight lows in the 30s.",
